  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 4♥. South has no reason to duck West's opening spade lead. After winning the ♠A, South must decide whether to take the heart finesse or the diamond finesse. There are certain losers in spades and clubs. South must avoid losing tricks to both the ♥K and the ♦K. As dummy has only one entry, South must immediately decide which finesse to take.\n\nOn the above lie of the cards, a successful heart finesse will not help. If the ♥Q is played from dummy, East will duck. A second heart finesse will leave East with a certain trump trick and South will still have to lose a diamond trick.\n\nTaking a heart finesse requires more than East having the ♥K. East must also have fewer than 4 hearts. Taking the diamond finesse requires only that East hold the ♦K. No additional break is required.\n\nSouth should therefore finesse diamonds at trick 2. If West follows small, South can drive out the ♥K and easily make the contract. If West wins the ♦K, South still has a small chance that the ♥K is singleton.",
